    "lawCode": "Amd §2801-a, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5555\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the public health law, in relation to allowing limited\nliability companies to operate renal disease diagnostic or treatment\ncenters\n\nPURPOSE:\nTo permit limited liability companies (LLCs), not just corporations,\nto operate dialysis related treatment centers.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nThe bill amends section 2801-a of the Public Health Law to add LLCs to\nthe law allowing corporations to operate renal disease (dialysis)\ndiagnostic or treatment centers.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nIn 2008, the state legislature recognized the need for more investment\nin renal dialysis services and amended Public Health law to permit\nthe operation of diagnostic and treatment centers offering\ndialysis-related services by publicly-traded corporations. Under the\ndirect oversight of the Department of Health, such corporations and\ntheir principal stockholders or members must demonstrate sufficient\nexperience and expertise in delivering high quality end-stage\nrenal disease care. The provisions of Article 28 in relation to\nquality monitoring, incident reporting, and enforcement apply to\nthese facilities.\n\nThe merits of the 2008 law apply equally to clinics whose owners have\nchosen the LLC form of business organization. In keeping with the\nintent of the 2008 law, LLCs should be permitted to qualify under\nArticle 28 to operate renal disease diagnostic or treatment centers,\non the same basis as corporations. The provisions of this section\napplicable to a corporation, including as to it's shareholders, would\napply to an LLC and its members.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNew bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone to the state.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n\n",
